name air-bomber-intercept
timeout 400

# THE OTHER INTERCEPT GEOMETRY, the one doc/modules/air/module.md carries as a case of its own: a
# FIGHTER AGAINST A BOMBER. It is not a duel with a slower opponent — the target does not manoeuvre, does
# not look and does not shoot, so what decides is reach, closure and the weapon's envelope, and nothing
# the bomber does.
#
# THE CAST
#   floggr  hostile `mig23` on the SPREAD planform, T3, and the N003E export set the catalogue takes
#           because O1's anchor is the Syrian force: 52 km search, 39 km track, and 14 km head-on
#           LOOK-DOWN. Two R-24R — SARH, "comparable to the AIM-7 Sparrow" [T4] — so the shooter is
#           BOUND TO IMPACT (FBSeekerHandoverS = -1) and cannot defend while it supports. It is briefed
#           by a controller (`set brief_gci`), because a GCI interceptor has no net: T3 carries no
#           cooperative half at all, which is a DOCTRINE statement and not a gap.
#   bear    friendly `tu95`, a MOVER at 8 000 m on a straight leg at its published 710 km/h cruise. Its
#           50.1 m span is the row's decisive number everywhere else in the tree; here what decides is
#           that it holds a line. ITS TAIL TURRET IS NOT MODELLED (module.md A6) — a stern conversion
#           against a bomber that shoots back is a different problem, and it is the second thing to add.
#
# WHAT TO READ
#   telemetry.csv   eng_* (the seven-state intercept machine WITHOUT its cooperative half: no PPLI, no
#                   sort, no cover deferral — the MiG-29's own measured position), air_tier = 3,
#                   air_cues = the briefed pointing, and fc_* — the LIVE launch zone, with fc_tta_s = -1
#                   (this round never lets go) and fc_bound = 1 from the shot on
#   events.log      `gci BRAA` -> `radar RADAR_CONTACT unit=floggr` -> `RADAR_DESIGNATE` ->
#                   `sms RELEASE` + `LAUNCH_SOLUTION` -> `missile PHASE` -> `ILLUMINATION_LOST`
#
# READING RULE — AND IT CHANGED AGAIN ON 2026-07-29, because this file has now recorded three different
# failures and the third is the one it exists for. The two it has retired stay written down, because a
# measured failure is knowledge:
#
#   2026-07-27  exit 2, `CRASH ground penetration` at t = 242 s. The MiG-23 held its commanded 330 kt
#               CAS with the THROTTLE AT IDLE and paid for it out of altitude, 8 000 m down to 510 m at
#               1 g. Cause: every catalogue row flew on systems/FBFlightControl's MiG-29 gain preset.
#               Fixed by the per-row preset (recipe §6.1, FBFlightControl::Raw).
#   2026-07-28  exit 3, TIMEOUT at 8 002.8 m. The aeroplane flew and the two aircraft passed head-on
#               without the MiG-23's own set ever firming the bomber. Attributed here to the intercept
#               GEOMETRY. THAT ATTRIBUTION WAS WRONG, and this file was the evidence: `set brief_gci 90`
#               is a TRUE bearing, FBAirModule entered it as a BODY-RELATIVE one, and a +-30 deg antenna
#               parked 90 deg off the nose sees nothing however good the geometry is. Nothing ever
#               re-centred the azimuth, because the intercept machine commands elevation only.
#   2026-07-29  the file's own subject, and it is a WEAPON result at last.
#
# IS: exit 3, TIMEOUT. The MiG-23 acquires at t = 114.0 s / 27.4 nm, designates at t = 153.1 s /
# 19.95 nm and RELEASES an R-24R at t = 195.3 s at 20.78 km — `ttaS = -1`, the semi-active round that
# BINDS ITS SHOOTER TO IMPACT. At t = 212.3 s, 16.9 s into a predicted 26.8 s time of flight, the
# MiG-23 loses the track and `missile ILLUMINATION_LOST` follows within the tick: the uplink stops
# because the fire control's own target estimate went invalid, and the round flies the rest of its
# life at nothing. THE SHOT IS SPOILED BY THE BREAK IN THE GUIDANCE, and that is the measurement
# air-eagle-sparrow.fbm is the positive half of.
#
# WHY THE TRACK BREAKS, and it is a SOURCED property rather than a defect: the N003E's elevation field
# is +-6 deg. The bomber is 1 000 m ABOVE the interceptor, so its elevation angle grows as they close —
# +1.15 deg at 50 km, +6.9 deg at 8.3 km — and it climbs out of the antenna's own window at exactly the
# range the log reports. An interceptor that wants this kill has to be vectored CO-ALTITUDE.
#
# What this file does NOT measure is whether the MiG-23's DECK is right — `make -C sim test-air mig23`
# prints that anchor by anchor (today: one of seven outside, the service ceiling, and the cause is the
# declared spread-planform choice).

unit floggr
  team hostile
  module mig23
  spawn 46.90000 6.30000 8000 90.0 480
  set gear up
  set task intercept
  set brief_gci 90 70 8000            # the controller's call: bearing 090, 70 km, co-altitude
  set store 1 r24r
  set store 2 r24r
  set brief_master_arm arm
  wp 46.90000 7.90000 8000 480

unit bear
  team friendly
  module tu95
  spawn 46.90000 7.60000 9000 270.0 380
  wp 46.90000 5.90000 9000 380
  objective survive
